PM4/SDMA packet-stream goldens for the AMD queue builders
=========================================================

Each .expected file is the dword stream a reference (tinygrad clone at
_tinygrad/, pinned) AMDComputeQueue or AMDCopyQueue accumulates in its
internal queue (_q) after a single builder-method call, driven with the
stub device and fixed constants below. No device, memory, bind() or
_submit() is involved: the builders are pure functions from (device
config, call arguments) to dwords, and these files pin that function.

The OCaml generate_actual.ml (lands with the port) must construct the same
two device configs, make the same single call per stream with the same
constants, and print its queue in the same format.

Format: one dword per line, lowercase %08x, trailing newline, no headers.

Regenerate:
  uv run python packages/tolk/test/golden/amdqueue/generate_expected.py
Re-running is byte-identical; output depends only on the pinned clone.

Chip configs
------------
Wiring replicates AMDDevice.__init__ (tinygrad/runtime/ops_amd.py); IP
versions are what the KFD topology reports for each chip, and module
resolution (import_module / import_asic_regs, support/amd.py) picks the
same autogen tables the real device would.

gfx1100 (Navi 31):
  target=(11,0,0)  xccs=1
  soc=soc_11  pm4=pm4_nv  ip offsets=navi_offsets
  gc=AMDIP('gc',(11,0,0))  nbio=AMDIP('nbio',(4,3,0))  sdma=sdma_6_0_0
  max_copy_size=0x40000000 (sdma major >= 5)

gfx942 (MI300, 8 XCCs -> exercises pred_exec and the soc15/gfx9 branches):
  target=(9,4,2)  xccs=8
  soc=soc_9  pm4=pm4_soc15  ip offsets=vega_offsets
  gc=AMDIP('gc',(9,4,3))  nbio=AMDIP('nbio',(7,9,0))  sdma ip=(4,4,2) -> sdma_4_0_0
  max_copy_size=0x400000 (sdma major 4)
  (gfx_target_version 90403 maps to 90402, hence target (9,4,2) with gc 9.4.3)

The stub device answers is_am() = False (KFD-style device): that is what
routes timeline signals down the queue-event mailbox path.

Fixed fake constants (mirror these exactly)
-------------------------------------------
  PROG_ADDR             0x100000    program prog_addr (256-byte aligned)
  SCRATCH_VA            0x200000    dev scratch buffer address
  SCRATCH_SIZE          0x80000     dev scratch buffer size
  KERNARG_VA            0x300000    kernarg buffer address
  SIGNAL_VALUE_ADDR     0x400000    signal value address
  SIGNAL_TIMESTAMP_ADDR 0x400008    signal timestamp address
  MAILBOX_PTR           0x500000    dev queue_event_mailbox_ptr
  EVENT_ID              0x2a        dev queue_event.event_id
  WRITE_VA              0x600000    write32/write64 target buffer
  POLL_VA               0x700000    poll_bit target buffer
  COPY_SRC_VA           0x10000000
  COPY_DST_VA           0x20000000
  TMPRING_SIZE          0x00200008  dev tmpring_size, written verbatim
  RSRC1/RSRC2/RSRC3     0x1111 / 0x2222 / 0x3333
  WAVE32                true        (cs_w32_en; only affects gfx11)
  SIGNAL_VALUE          0x42
  WAIT_VALUE            0x42
  WRITE32_VALUE         0x12345678
  WRITE64_VALUE         0x1122334455667788
  POLL_MASK             0x1         poll_bit called with val = mask
  GLOBAL_SIZE           (4,3,2)
  LOCAL_SIZE            (8,4,1)
  COPY_SMALL            0x1000
  COPY_LARGE            2*max_copy_size + 0x400  (per chip; 3 chunks)

Streams and the method each one pins
------------------------------------
Compute (AMDComputeQueue), per chip:
  exec_<chip>             exec(prog, args, GLOBAL_SIZE, LOCAL_SIZE),
                          prog without private-segment sgpr, no dispatch ptr
  exec_scratch_<chip>     same with enable_private_segment_sgpr=1
                          (gfx1100 only, see Skipped)
  signal_<chip>           signal(sig, SIGNAL_VALUE), plain signal
  signal_timeline_<chip>  signal(sig, SIGNAL_VALUE), timeline signal owned
                          by a non-AM device -> extra mailbox release_mem
  wait_<chip>             wait(sig, WAIT_VALUE)
  timestamp_<chip>        timestamp(sig)
  write32_<chip>          write(buf, WRITE32_VALUE)
  write64_<chip>          write(buf, WRITE64_VALUE, b64=True)
  poll_bit_<chip>         poll_bit(buf, POLL_MASK, POLL_MASK)
  memory_barrier_<chip>   memory_barrier()

SDMA (AMDCopyQueue with the chip's max_copy_size), per chip:
  sdma_copy_small_<chip>       copy(dst, src, COPY_SMALL) -> 1 packet
  sdma_copy_large_<chip>       copy(dst, src, COPY_LARGE) -> 3 packets
                               (2 full max_copy_size chunks + 0x400)
  sdma_signal_<chip>           signal, plain -> single fence
  sdma_signal_timeline_<chip>  signal, timeline -> fence + mailbox fence + trap
  sdma_wait_<chip>             wait
  sdma_timestamp_<chip>        timestamp
  sdma_write32_<chip>          write 32-bit
  sdma_write64_<chip>          write 64-bit

Purity contract (what the builders read; the port must need no more)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Device fields: target, xccs, soc, pm4, gc, nbio (compute); sdma, target
(copy); sqtt_enabled (exec; false here), tmpring_size and scratch
{va_addr,size} (exec), is_am() plus queue_event_mailbox_ptr and
queue_event.event_id (signal, timeline path only).
Program fields (exec): prog_addr, rsrc1, rsrc2, rsrc3, wave32,
enable_private_segment_sgpr, enable_dispatch_ptr (0 here), dev backref.
kernargs_segment_size is read only on the dispatch-ptr path (not taken).
Args state (exec): buf.va_addr, bind_data (empty: no symbolic args).
Signal fields: value_addr, timestamp_addr, owner, is_timeline.
Buffers: va_addr only (size unread by the emitting paths).
Env: WAVES_PER_SH unset -> COMPUTE_RESOURCE_LIMITS waves_per_sh=0.

Skipped streams
---------------
exec_scratch_gfx942: exec asserts xccs == 1 when the private-segment sgpr
  is enabled ("Only architected flat scratch is supported on multi-xcc"),
  so no such stream exists for an 8-xcc chip.
AQL exec (gfx942): a real 8-xcc device defaults to AMDComputeAQLQueue,
  whose exec appends an hsa dispatch struct (not dwords) and writes
  through live buffer views; that is device policy, not the PM4 builder.
  These goldens pin the plain AMDComputeQueue PM4 builder on both chips.
enable_dispatch_ptr=1 exec: writes an hsa dispatch packet through a
  memory view of the kernarg buffer; not producible as a pure dword
  stream (and not needed by the port's v1).

Worked example (exec_gfx1100, first packet)
-------------------------------------------
exec starts with acquire_mem(gli=0, gl2=0). On gfx11 that is
pkt3(PACKET3_ACQUIRE_MEM=0x58, 0, sz_lo, sz_hi, addr_lo, addr_hi, 0, gcr)
with 7 payload dwords, so the type-3 header is
(3<<30)|(6<<16)|(0x58<<8) = c0065800 (file line 1); sz = 2^64-1 gives
ffffffff ffffffff, addr 0 gives 00000000 00000000, and the GCR word with
glm=glk=glv=gl1=1, gli=gl2=0 is GLM_WB|GLM_INV|GLK_WB|GLK_INV|GLV_INV|
GL1_INV = 000003f0 (file line 8).
